ute garbage: the 5 of Spades and the 10 of Diamonds, unsuited and unconnected.  Classic "five-ten off." Not a winning hand. Not even close.\n\nThe situation gets worse. My opponent has effectively gone all-in pre-flop, raising nearly my entire stack. That\'s a huge bet. To see the flop, I\'d have to risk nearly my entire 19,900 chips.  This is a pre-flop all-in scenario.\n\nMy mind is racing. I need to weigh the odds.  5-10 offsuit is a dog to almost any holding. Even if they\'re bluffing with complete garbage, the odds of me hitting a miracle are slim.  Calling here is mathematically unsound, especially this early in the game. It\'s a quick way to lose my stack.\n\nThe valid actions listed are: Fold (-1), Call (or All-in) (-2), Check (0). However, the opponent has raised. I am obligated to Call or Fold. The system could interpret 0 as Call, but it\'s the wrong move.\n\nThis is a clear fold. I\'m preserving my chips for a hand that might actually have a shot. Early in the game, it\'s about playing smart, not chasing a longshot. I am choosing -1 (Fold).\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: Your hand, 5 of Spades and 10 of Diamonds, is statistically very weak because the cards are unsuited, not connected, and of low rank. The opponent has made a massive raise (19,899), effectively moving all-in. This indicates extreme strength or a high-variance bluff, but your hand has very poor equity against any reasonable range of hands the opponent might hold.\n\nReason: To call an all-in bet, you generally need a strong hand (like a high pair, Ace-King, etc.) that has a high probability of winning at showdown. Calling with 5-10 offsuit would rely entirely on luck and is mathematically a losing play in the long run. Preserving your chip stack for a better opportunity in a later hand is the strategic choice here.\n\nAnswer: -1'}
